<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Yucatán</h1><p class="st-meta">1841 CE – 1848 CE · capital Mérida</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Breakaway republic on the Yucatán Peninsula that twice left Mexico in the 1840s and returned in 1848 as the Caste War threatened its existence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yucatán's elites resented the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/centralist-republic-of-mexico">Centralist Republic of Mexico</a> and its taxes and levies. A federalist revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-iman">Santiago Imán</a> in 1839 to 1840 restored local autonomy, and in 1841 the peninsula declared itself independent, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merida-yucatan">Mérida</a> as capital. A brief reunion in 1843 broke down, and Yucatán separated again in 1845 to 1846.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its 1841 constitution, drafted largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-crescencio-rejon">Manuel Crescencio Rejón</a>, introduced the amparo, a citizen's legal protection against unconstitutional acts that later entered Mexican law. Politics was dominated by the rivalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-mendez">Santiago Méndez</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-barbachano">Miguel Barbachano</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merida-yucatan">Mérida</a>. The economy relied on henequen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and Maya peasants bore heavy taxes, debt and loss of land, while being armed for the factions' wars.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1847 Maya communities rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste-war-of-yucatan">Caste War</a> and nearly drove the whites from the peninsula. Desperate, the government offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> sent troops and money, and Yucatán formally rejoined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in August 1848. Rebel Maya later founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chan-santa-cruz">Chan Santa Cruz</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-yucatan.jpg" alt="Republic of Yucatán" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Yucatan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1841 CE – 1848 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mérida</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Santiago Méndez, Miguel Barbachano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish and Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Yucatan, República de Yucatán</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=republic-of-yucatan" aria-label="Republic of Yucatán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2270 1402 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Yucatán lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2590 1658L2321 1717M2590 1658L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2590 1658h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mérida. <a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=republic-of-yucatan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
